ewhere retain 39-53% of edges, so this is not the group's normal churn. Activity volume and per-node degree are stable across the boundary, excluding a burst/dropout artifact, and a non-member control group shows flat 0.93-0.99 turnover with no peak at 77986, excluding graph-wide rewiring as the explanation."}, "raw_final_response": "All evidence gathered. Here is my analysis:\n\n**Signature of an abrupt regime switch, confirmed against controls:**\n\n1. **Baseline vs. peak (step_01):** The group's turnover sits at a *low, stable* 0.21\u20130.32 for most of the period \u2014 meaning a persistent, recurring contact set \u2014 then rises sharply to **1.0** at t\u224877986, holds through ~80551, and relaxes back to 0.21\u20130.26. This is a spike against the group's own baseline, not drift.\n\n2. **Complete contact replacement (step_03):** Diffing \u00b15000 around t=77986 gives **Jaccard = 0.0**: all 138 edges retired, 77 new edges formed, zero survivors. The old cohort {170, 172, 195, 309, 325, 337, 508, 519, 593, 694, 922, 932, 1044, 1105, 1635, 1897} is wholly replaced by {272, 426, 460, 462, 474, 834, 901, 1091, 1154, 1416, 1437, 1520, 1619, 1743}. Crucially the switch is **coordinated across all 12 members** \u2014 each member independently adopts partners from the same new set.\n\n3. **Not ordinary turnover for this group (steps 06, 07):** Equivalent \u00b15000 diffs elsewhere in the window yield Jaccard **0.39** (64269\u219274270) and **0.53** (84269\u219294269). The group normally retains 40\u201350% of its edges across an adjacent split; only at 77986 does retention collapse to zero.\n\n**Confounders ruled out:**\n\n- *Volume artifact:* Global activity is undisturbed across the boundary (207/122/183 events, step_09); the group's own rate is comparable before and after (step_04 vs step_05). The switch is compositional, not a burst or dropout.\n- *Graph-wide churn:* The global diff also reads Jaccard 0 (step_08), so I tested a non-member control group (step_14, step_15). Those nodes show turnover pinned at **0.93\u20130.99 flat across the entire period with no peak at 77986** \u2014 background nodes simply have non-repeating partners. The review group is the only one with a low stable baseline that discontinuously jumps.\n- *Popularity/lifespan:* Old partners are near-absent after the switch and new partners near-absent before (steps 10, 11), consistent with a genuine substitution of contact regimes rather than the group tracking whichever nodes are currently popular.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_798d04c6\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [121, 357, 381, 594, 728, 785, 898, 1269, 1415, 1460, 1701, 1923],\n  \"window\": [64269, 94269],\n  \"confidence\": 0.88,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The 12-node group shows a sharp regime switch at t\u224877986. Its neighbourhood turnover holds a low, stable baseline (0.21-0.32) indicating a persistent contact cohort, then spikes to 1.0 and returns to baseline. A \u00b15000 diff at that point yields Jaccard 0.0: all 138 prior edges vanish and 77 new ones appear, with every member simultaneously switching from the old cohort (170,172,309,694,1044,1897,...) to a new one (460,901,1091,1154,1619,...). Adjacent-period diffs elsewhere retain 39-53% of edges, so this is not the group's normal churn. Activity volume and per-node degree are stable across the boundary, excluding a burst/dropout artifact, and a non-member control group shows flat 0.93-0.99 turnover with no peak at 77986, excluding graph-wide rewiring as the explanation.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 66397, "total_output_tokens": 5877, "total_tokens": 72274, "total_latency_ms": 85042, "llm_calls": 7, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}